<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander I of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexander I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from 1801 to 1825. He led Russia through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, including the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion in 1812, and Russian armies reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814. He took a central part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, and his early reforming plans gave way to more conservative rule later in his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Russia" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Russia_by_G.Dawe_(1826,_Peterhof)-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The central apparatus changed over the imperial period: Peter I's collegiate system of administrative boards (1718) gave way under <b>Alexander I</b> to eight ministries (1802), which divided executive authority more rationally by function.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Many officers were at first encouraged by the early liberal reforms of Tsar <b>Alexander I</b>, when liberalism was officially encouraged and expectations ran high.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Three emperors ruled in these decades: <b>Alexander I</b> (1801–1825), who drove Napoleon’s army out of Russia in 1812; his brother Nicholas I (1825–1855), who began his reign by crushing the Decembrist revolt of noble officers; and Nicholas’s son Alexander II (1855–1881), who freed the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rainers were singing it around Christmas 1819, once performed it before an audience that included Franz I of Austria and <b>Alexander I of Russia</b>, and gave its first performance in the United States, in New York City in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon and Emperor <b>Alexander I of Russia</b> signed the first of the Treaties of Tilsit, ending hostilities between France and Russia in the War of the Fourth Coalition after the French victory at Friedland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Alexander I</b>, aware of the plot, succeeded him and later led Russia against Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-23">23 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Taganrog, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1808" aria-label="Saint Petersburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5185 154 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander I of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5635 424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5635 424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1808">What was here in 1808? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
